  "query_preview": "High water for back Bays across Southern Delaware are a \nconcern too, so a Coastal Flood Warnings for Sussex County Delaware \nis in effect until Monday 2 PM.  \n\nFor Delaware Bay communities, a strengthening Northwest wind will \nremain across Delaware Bay into tonight. This will promote lowering \ntidal departures as the waters is pushed down/out Delaware Bay. As a \nresult, additional flooding (at high tides) will be spotty Minor or \nless. No additional flags for tidal flooding are expected. \n\nFor the tidal Delaware River, the North to Northeast flow has kept \nwater levels (at high tide) at the lower part of Minor flooding \nrange. No flags for tidal flooding are expected. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dangerous rip currents and high surf are expected at \nthe Jersey Shore and Delaware Beaches through Sunday. Additional \nbeach and dune erosion can be expected.\n\nA nor'easter is bringing strong north to northeast winds and \nhigh seas for our marine zones. Breaking waves within the surf \nzone of 6 to 12 feet will occur through Sunday. Dangerous rip \ncurrents will accompany the high surf, making it extremely \ndangerous to enter the water at any time through Sunday. The \nhigh surf and strong winds are resulting in areas of beach \nerosion across the Jersey Shore and Delaware Beaches. Across \nsome locales, large breaking waves and run- up are also eroding \ndunes.\n\nThe High Risk for life threatening rip currents remains in \neffect through Sunday for all beaches. A High Surf Advisory is \nin effect for all beaches through Sunday as well.\n\nThe risk for dangerous rip currents will begin to decrease on \nMonday as winds shift offshore and wave heights begin to \ndecrease. For now have opted for a MODERATE risk for all shore \npoints.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...A cold front is likely to cross through the region \nFriday into Saturday.\n\nThe surface high that will be over the region for much of the week \nwill start to build southeast and off shore by Thursday. This will \nopen the door for a cold front to sweep through the region Friday or \nSaturday. Models for the most part, aren't depicting a widespread \nrain event with this front. However, southwesterly flow on Wednesday \nand Thursday, should set the stage with ample moisture in place. The \nbiggest question is forcing with this front. The best synoptic scale \nlift will likely be well removed from the mesoscale forcing \nassociated with the front. For now the 20 to 30 percent PoPs through \nthis period seem appropriate this far out. Preliminary guidance \nindicates that temperatures behind the front could be 5 to 10 \ndegrees colder than normal.",
